            },
            "createdOn": 1780473825,
            "author": {
                "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                "username": "jagpalgill"
            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 177,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Doesn't this solution retain the issue that `createDevice` can run multiple times  for the same configuration object path?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 177,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, createDevice can/may be called multiple times, but the other steps in caller function (inventory associations etc) will only run once now. In general, i don't see why we will have issues with it being called multiple times.\n\n@angelaptcheng@gmail.com Can you please be more specific if you were seeing any issues with calling initDevice multiple times?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 177,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                        "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                        "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
                    },
                    "message": "The issue we observed was with Yosemite5_SCM_CPLD. initDevice() was entered twice for the same configuration path before the first call completed.\n\nThe duplicated \"Missing property Name\" / \"Missing property Polarity\" messages show that the same configuration path was processed twice. Both calls then tried to set up the same GPIO mux. The first call requested DEBUG_CARD_BYPASS and continued reading the CPLD version, while the second call failed because the same GPIO line was still in use:\n```\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Missing property Name on path /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/board/Yosemite5_SCM_2700/Yosemite5_SCM_CPLD, interface xyz.openbmc_project.Configuration.LatticeLCMXO3D_9400Firmware.MuxOutputs1\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Missing property Name on path /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/board/Yosemite5_SCM_2700/Yosemite5_SCM_CPLD, interface xyz.openbmc_project.Configuration.LatticeLCMXO3D_9400Firmware.MuxOutputs1\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Missing property Polarity on path /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/board/Yosemite5_SCM_2700/Yosemite5_SCM_CPLD, interface xyz.openbmc_project.Configuration.LatticeLCMXO3D_9400Firmware.MuxOutputs1\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Requesting chip gpiochip1, GPIO line DEBUG_CARD_BYPASS to 1\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Getting Lattice CPLD version\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Missing property Polarity on path /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/board/Yosemite5_SCM_2700/Yosemite5_SCM_CPLD, interface xyz.openbmc_project.Configuration.LatticeLCMXO3D_9400Firmware.MuxOutputs1\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: GPIO line DEBUG_CARD_BYPASS was still used\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Failed to mux GPIOs\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Failed to setup mux: Failed to mux GPIOs to BMC\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Failed to get CPLD version: unable to acquire mux\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: CPLD version: 00040D04\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Requesting chip gpiochip1, GPIO line DEBUG_CARD_BYPASS to 0\nMay 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Failed to get CPLD version for Yosemite5_SCM_CPLD\n```\nAs a result, the successful read returned 00040D04, but the other concurrent initialization failed and the SCM CPLD version was stored as \"unknown\"."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 177,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "@angelaptcheng@gmail.com I see this other issue apart from the issue u r trying to solve. So, lets understand this too -\n\n > May 18 06:05:19 bmc phosphor-cpld-software-update[1104]: Missing property Polarity on path /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/board/Yosemite5_SCM_2700/Yosemite5_SCM_CPLD, interface xyz.openbmc_project.Configuration.LatticeLCMXO3D_9400Firmware.MuxOutputs1\n\nWhy are we seeing this when the polarity is already defined -https://grok.openbmc.org/xref/openbmc/entity-manager/configurations/meta/yv5/yosemite5_scm_2700.json?r=deb2c37a1836aeb93c1b54289ea2e13f77eaad23#295 \n\n\nIn general, the fix i gave you won't solve the gpio failure issue and the way i see code it continues even if it fails to read the version (which is again a separate issue).\n\nBut for now lets try to get answer to my above questions and restart work on your patch again."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 177,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                        "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                        "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
                    },
                    "message": "The \"Missing property Name/Polarity\" messages are produced by the current probing logic in CPLDSoftwareManager::initDevice().\n\nThe loop checks MuxOutputs0, MuxOutputs1, MuxOutputs2, ... until it reaches an entry that does not exist:\n```\n    for (size_t i = 0; true; i++)\n    {\n        const std::string iface = configIfaceMux + std::to_string(i);\n\n        std::optional<std::string> name =\n            co_await dbusGetRequiredProperty<std::string>(..., iface, \"Name\");\n\n        std::optional<std::string> polarity =\n            co_await dbusGetRequiredProperty<std::string>(..., iface, \"Polarity\");\n\n        if (!name.has_value() || !polarity.has_value())\n        {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        names.push_back(name.value());\n        values.push_back((polarity == \"High\") ? 1 : 0);\n    }\n```\ndbusGetRequiredProperty() logs an error when the property is missing. So when the loop probes the first non-existent MuxOutputsN, it logs the missing Name/Polarity message and then exits the loop.\n\nIn this case, Polarity is defined for the existing MuxOutputs entry. The log comes from probing the next non-existent MuxOutputs entry, not from the existing configuration.\n\nAlso, we confirmed your observation that this approach does not address the GPIO failure itself. We still reproduced the GPIO failure while testing it on our machine."
                }
            ],
